# =============================================================================
# SENTINEL — BUILD CONTEXT EXCLUSIONS. The repository's first .dockerignore.
#
# This file is a SECURITY BOUNDARY, not a build-speed optimisation, and it is
# written deny-by-default for that reason. A denylist is a list of the secrets
# somebody happened to think of; the first file this repository grows that is
# secret in a shape nobody anticipated — a support engineer's `dump.sql`, a
# `.p8` from a mobile lane, a `kubeconfig` — would be silently uploaded to the
# daemon and, if any COPY were ever widened, silently baked into a layer that
# ships to a wiring closet.
#
# So: EXCLUDE EVERYTHING, then admit by name exactly the workspace files
# `services/edge-runtime/Dockerfile` needs, then re-exclude the dangerous
# things that live INSIDE those admitted directories. Later patterns win.
#
# Adding a file to this repository can never widen the image by accident. It
# can only be admitted by an edit to this file, which is a reviewable diff.
# =============================================================================

# ---- 1. Nothing is in the context unless named below. -----------------------
*

# ---- 2. Workspace metadata pnpm needs to resolve a frozen, filtered install. -
!package.json
!pnpm-workspace.yaml
!pnpm-lock.yaml
!tsconfig.base.json

# ---- 3. The only two workspace members Edge is built from. ------------------
# NOT apps/, NOT services/core-api, NOT services/*, NOT tests/, NOT docs/,
# NOT .github/, NOT scripts/, NOT infrastructure/ — none of which are excluded
# by a rule naming them, but by never having been admitted in the first place.
# .git is in that same set: the whole history, every branch, and every secret
# ever committed and later removed, is the single largest thing a careless
# container build leaks. It is not here.
!packages/contracts
!services/edge-runtime

# ---- 4. Re-exclusions INSIDE the two admitted directories. ------------------
# Everything below is a real file that exists, or will exist, under those paths.

# Host-built artefacts. `node_modules` from a Windows/macOS dev machine carries
# platform-specific binaries that would silently poison a linux/amd64 image;
# `dist` from a dev machine is code nobody can attribute to a commit. Both are
# rebuilt inside the build stage from the lockfile, which is the point.
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/build
**/coverage
**/.turbo
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Package-manager caches. A pnpm/npm store inside a layer is tens of megabytes
# of content-addressed tarballs that the runtime cannot use and an attacker can
# read for the exact dependency inventory of the box.
**/.pnpm-store
**/.npm
**/node_modules/.cache

# TEST TOOLING. Specs never run in the image, and one of these is not merely
# dead weight: `edge-trusted-time.test-support.ts` GENERATES P-256 ANCHOR
# SIGNING KEYPAIRS. It is excluded from `tsconfig`'s spec exclusion because it
# is not a `.spec.ts`, so without this line `tsc` would compile it and a
# working anchor-forging helper would ship inside every Edge image — on a box
# whose entire security story is that it CANNOT author its own trusted time.
# Nothing outside a spec imports it, so its absence cannot break the build.
**/*.spec.ts
**/*.test.ts
**/*.test-support.ts
**/vitest.config.*
**/__tests__
**/__fixtures__

# SECRETS. Belt and braces: nothing under the two admitted directories should
# ever be a key, and if one appears there anyway it does not reach the daemon.
# The Edge signing key and the trusted-time keyring arrive by MOUNT at runtime
# (see the Dockerfile's custody section); neither has any business in a layer.
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.p8
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/*.jks
**/*.keystore
**/id_rsa*
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
**/secrets
**/*.secret

# Editor / OS noise that would otherwise bust the layer cache on every commit.
**/.DS_Store
**/Thumbs.db
**/*.swp
**/.idea
**/.vscode

# The Dockerfile itself. It is read by the frontend before the context is
# assembled, so it is never needed inside it.
services/edge-runtime/Dockerfile
